INT. ABANDONED OPERA HOUSE - STAGE - NIGHT

Torn velvet curtains. A dusty chandelier. Moonlight through a hole in the dome.

ELIAS VANTERPOOL, 50s, in a rumpled suit, stands center stage with a flashlight, scanning the empty rows. He is breathing hard, bleeding from a cut above his eye.

ELIAS
(to the dark)
I know you're out there.

No answer. The chandelier creaks. He turns the beam on the box seats.

ELIAS (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Steady. Steady.

A seat folds down with a clack. Elias spins. Nothing. A whisper drifts through the hall, impossible to place.

A woman steps from the wings, slowly, hands visible: ODETTE MARCHAND-OYELARAN, 40s, in a long gray coat, a small revolver in her gloved hand. She does not raise it.

ODETTE
Hello, Elias.

ELIAS
Odette. How did you find me?

ODETTE
You always loved this place.

ELIAS
(to the empty house)
All of you, stay back!

ODETTE
There's no one here but us.

ELIAS
Then why did you bring the gun?

ODETTE
Habit.

She holds it up and then tosses it. It clatters center stage between them. Elias looks at it. He does not move.

ELIAS
What do you want?

ODETTE
The ledger.

ELIAS
I burned it.

ODETTE
You didn't.

ELIAS
(quietly, to himself)
I should have.

He pulls a thick notebook from inside his coat and holds it out. She doesn't take it. The chandelier shivers.

ODETTE
Keep it. I only needed to know you still had it.

ELIAS
Why?

ODETTE
Because they're coming for it. And now they know too.

A door slams somewhere in the dark. Elias's face drains. Odette steps back into the wings.

ODETTE (CONT'D)
Run, Elias.

He drops the flashlight. It rolls into the orchestra pit. Footsteps pound from every direction. Elias bolts upstage.
